# Nearby People And Local Discussions

Status: implemented on `nearby-discovery` for testing; both rollout switches remain off by default.

Review the user-facing copy during manual testing. Keep both rollout switches off by default so deployment changes no existing behavior.

## Confirmed product decisions

- Use exact shared coordinates only for private distance calculation.
- The freely typed profile city remains display-only and must never be used for nearby matching.
- Never show coordinates or exact distance to another user or discussion creator.
- Do not sort results by exact distance.
- Use one configurable nearby radius for People and Discussions.
- Initial radius: strictly less than 50 km.
- Locations do not expire automatically.
- Users must be able to share, update, and remove their location.
- Removing a location removes the user from location-based matching.
- Existing stored locations must not automatically opt users into Nearby after rollout.
- Existing “Show me in See People / Hide me” controls whether a profile participates in Nearby People.
- Keep Nearby available without premium access initially so the discovery pool is not artificially reduced.
- Use two independent dashboard switches:
  - Nearby People
  - Local Discussions
- Both switches default off.
- Location controls are available when at least one Nearby feature is enabled.

## Nearby People behavior

- Start from the existing normal People candidate pool.
- Preserve all current eligibility, privacy, ban, language, saved-profile, deduplication, and taste-relevance rules.
- A viewer needs a valid shared location and must be visible in See People before Nearby ranking is applied.
- A candidate counts as nearby only when:
  - the candidate has a valid shared location;
  - the candidate is visible in See People;
  - the viewer is visible in See People; and
  - the private calculated distance is below the configured radius.
- Partition the already-eligible results into:
  1. nearby candidates;
  2. all other normal candidates.
- Show nearby candidates first.
- Preserve the existing People relevance/order inside both groups.
- Add the bot-controlled `📍` badge only to nearby candidates.
- Do not expose distance bands, kilometers, coordinates, direction, or a map.
- Non-nearby and location-unavailable candidates remain visible afterward in their normal order.
- When Nearby People is disabled globally or by the viewer, People ordering and rendering remain exactly as they are now.

## Local Discussion creation

- Add an optional `📍 Make local` action to the final discussion preview.
- Show it only when the Local Discussions dashboard switch is enabled.
- If the creator has no valid shared location, ask them to share location before making the discussion local.
- Use the creator's current shared location.
- Save a fixed location snapshot on the discussion when it becomes local.
- Do not follow later creator-location updates.
- The final preview must clearly show whether the discussion is local without displaying its coordinates.
- Allow the creator to remove the local setting before confirming creation.
- Confirm whether an already-created discussion may later add/remove its local status; implement only after that edit behavior is explicitly approved.

## Local Discussion discovery

- Start from the existing normal Active Discussions candidate pool.
- Preserve all current open/full, membership, join-rule, removed-user, language, relevance-tier, boost, freshness, capacity, and starter-room rules.
- A local discussion counts as nearby only when:
  - it has a valid creator-location snapshot;
  - the viewer has a valid shared location; and
  - the private calculated distance is below the configured radius.
- Partition already-eligible discussions into:
  1. nearby local discussions;
  2. all other normal discussions.
- Show nearby local discussions first.
- Preserve the existing discussion ranking inside both groups.
- Add the bot-controlled `📍` badge only when the discussion is nearby for that viewer.
- Do not sort by exact distance or expose coordinates, kilometers, direction, or maps.
- Discussions outside the radius and non-local discussions remain visible afterward normally.
- When Local Discussions is disabled, hide creation controls and apply no nearby badge or ranking.
- Keep stored local snapshots intact but unused while the feature is disabled.

## Location UX

- Audit the currently disabled profile-location flow and reuse its safe Telegram location input.
- Do not add another Nearby on/off setting. Reuse See People visibility.
- Agree on Persian and English copy before implementation for:
  - what Nearby does;
  - See People visibility;
  - private use of exact coordinates;
  - no public exact distance;
  - updating location;
  - removing location;
  - disabling Nearby while retaining or deleting location;
  - making a discussion local;
  - removing local status;
  - unavailable/invalid location;
  - no nearby results, if a dedicated state is shown.
- Keep all location actions together:
  - Share location
  - Update location
  - Remove location
- The location screen contains Share/Update, Remove when available, and Cancel.
- No automatic location expiry.
- Consider showing the user their last location-update date privately without exposing it publicly.

## Anti-spoofing and safety

- Keep `📍` reserved for bot-generated Nearby badges.
- Existing validation already rejects emoji in profile names, discussion titles, and discussion descriptions; add explicit regression tests for `📍`.
- Never send a nearby person's identity, location, or recipient list to a local discussion creator.
- Never reveal who was considered nearby but filtered out.
- Respect People visibility opt-out, bans, reports, invitation restrictions where applicable, and all existing discussion safety rules.
- Revalidate location, See People visibility, switches, discussion state, and eligibility at action time.
- Nearby does not bypass join rules, capacity, language, one-active-room, removed-user, or profile-name requirements.
- Do not describe Nearby as live presence or claim that someone is currently at the stored location.

## Data model and geospatial work

- Reuse `showProfileInPeopleDiscovery`; do not add a separate Nearby consent field.
- Reuse existing user latitude/longitude only after validating numeric bounds.
- Add a geospatial representation/index suitable for bounded distance queries without exposing coordinates in public rendering.
- Keep old users and existing location documents backward-compatible.
- Add optional local-discussion fields:
  - local enabled flag;
  - creator location snapshot/geospatial point;
  - snapshot timestamp.
- Do not derive local status from the creator's current profile after creation.
- Ensure account deletion and product-data cleanup remove all user location and local snapshot data required by privacy policy.
- Define behavior when a creator removes their profile location after creating a local discussion: the confirmed discussion snapshot remains until the discussion closes or local status is removed.

## Dashboard and rollout

- Add independent dashboard switches:
  - `nearbyPeopleEnabled`
  - `localDiscussionsEnabled`
- Add configurable `nearbyRadiusKm`, initially 50 km with strict `< radius` matching.
- Define safe minimum/maximum dashboard bounds for the radius.
- Both feature switches default off on deployment.
- When both are off:
  - hide all Nearby/local UI;
  - perform no geospatial matching;
  - preserve stored data without using it.
- Display the effective radius and both switch states in dashboard configuration/diagnostics.
- Keep premium gating out of the first implementation; revisit only after measuring adoption and nearby pool size.

## Performance

- Use indexed geospatial queries; never calculate distance against the full user or discussion collection in application memory.
- Apply normal eligibility filters before or alongside bounded geospatial matching.
- Keep query limits and pagination bounded.
- Avoid additional location queries when the relevant dashboard switch is off or the viewer is hidden from See People.
- Preserve deterministic People pagination and existing discussion ranking within nearby/non-nearby partitions.
- Define stable pagination behavior so repeated requests do not reshuffle results.

## Analytics and privacy-safe measurement

- Track only aggregate events:
  - Nearby enabled/disabled;
  - location shared/updated/removed;
  - nearby People result opened;
  - local discussion marked/unmarked;
  - nearby local discussion opened/joined.
- Never log coordinates, exact distance, or recipient/candidate location pairs.
- Add dashboard counts for location-sharing discoverable users and local discussions without exposing identities or locations.
- Measure whether Nearby improves profile opens, saved profiles, discussion previews, and joins before considering premium access.

## Tests

- User location validation and coordinate bounds.
- Existing stored location works only while the profile remains visible in See People.
- Share/update/remove behavior.
- Removing location removes the user from location matching.
- Dashboard switches independently hide/show their feature surfaces.
- Both switches off preserve current People and Discussion behavior exactly.
- Strict radius boundary: below radius is nearby; equal to or above is not.
- Nearby People requires both profiles to remain visible in See People.
- Nearby candidates appear first without distance sorting.
- Existing relevance order remains stable inside nearby and non-nearby groups.
- Non-nearby People still appear afterward.
- Local discussion uses a creation-time creator-location snapshot.
- Creator location updates do not move an existing local discussion.
- Nearby local discussions appear first while preserving current ranking inside groups.
- Outside-radius and non-local discussions still appear afterward.
- No exact location/distance appears in messages, profiles, callbacks, analytics, or logs.
- `📍` remains rejected in user-entered profile names, discussion titles, and descriptions.
- All existing join, invitation, block/restriction, visibility, pagination, and stale-action protections remain intact.
- Account deletion removes required location data.
- Translation validation, unit tests, integration tests for database mutations, and full regression suite.

